\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The measure dual on one- and two-dimensional instances (Theorem\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {thm:dual}, Theorem\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {thm:helly}, Proposition\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {prop:nonconvex}). (a) Two-floor instance: the lower envelope peaks at $-\genfrac  {}{}{}1{1}{10}$ and the equal-weight pooled row is the constant $-\genfrac  {}{}{}1{1}{10}$. (b) Feasible contrast: the envelope peaks at $+\genfrac  {}{}{}1{1}{10}$ and every pooled row (thin lines) is positive somewhere. (c) Parity instance on the non-convex $U=\{0,1\}$: the value over $U$ is $-1$, the value over $\operatorname  {conv}U$ is $0$, so no certificate exists although the atom is obstructed. (d) The tight family for $k=2$: three half-planes, pairwise consistent on $U=[0,2]^2$, with empty triple intersection; the lower value is $-\genfrac  {}{}{}1{1}{3}$ and the uniform measure is the unique optimal certificate.\relax }}{6}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:dual}{{1}{6}{The measure dual on one- and two-dimensional instances (Theorem~\ref {thm:dual}, Theorem~\ref {thm:helly}, Proposition~\ref {prop:nonconvex}). (a) Two-floor instance: the lower envelope peaks at $-\tfr 1{10}$ and the equal-weight pooled row is the constant $-\tfr 1{10}$. (b) Feasible contrast: the envelope peaks at $+\tfr 1{10}$ and every pooled row (thin lines) is positive somewhere. (c) Parity instance on the non-convex $U=\{0,1\}$: the value over $U$ is $-1$, the value over $\conv U$ is $0$, so no certificate exists although the atom is obstructed. (d) The tight family for $k=2$: three half-planes, pairwise consistent on $U=[0,2]^2$, with empty triple intersection; the lower value is $-\tfr 13$ and the uniform measure is the unique optimal certificate.\relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
